
In this extract we listen to an enslaved person discussing the loss of their slave name. This moment brings great uncertainty and is disconcerting to this character. There is a sense of them being institutionalised to the system that oppresses them. This contrasts with the character of Jim in *Huckleberry Finn* who runs away from slavery and wishes to escape. However, we can see comparisons with Mark Twain's careful voicing of Jim and the character here. The heavy uses of phonetic spelling and colloquialisms places both characters at a similar level of disadvantaged education due to their slaving background. While African-American characters are not prominent in others work I have studied they also provide a less direct comparison. The car of black passengers with white chauffeur Nick sees in *The Great Gatsby* shows a completely different life for black people in America, it is notable that *Gatsby* came many decades later. However, the absurdity of this idea to Nick shows a deeper set racial bias than the obvious form of oppression represented by slavery. Again, the character here could be contrasted by the characters of *Lola Leroy* by Frances E.W. Harper who seem ready and excited by the prospect of revolt. It is not to say this character supports the institution of slavery but instead fears the uncertainty to come with his life after. Most notably among the texts I studied the *Grapes of Wrath* by John Steinbeck fails to include black characters. This could be interpreted as a deliberate choice by Steinbeck to focus on the class struggle which he views as more important. The omission of the African-American experience creates a perhaps more hollow book for some. This extract produces a more nuanced and human character who is deeply pragmatic in his wish for progress, despite hatred for his master he recognises change is turbulent and unsettling something that must be considered relatable to the reader. 

