This extract opens on a surprising note for a modern reader, it begins with a more negative or mixed view on emancipation. One of the most notable things to the readers is the phonetic nature of the text. This works to give a sense of accuracy to the ideas presented that would perhaps clash with the audiences preconceived notions of emancipation. The use of the term 'quarest ting' within the first few words of the extract challenges the audience, freedom is not seen as purely virtuous but something difficult and something perhaps confusing. This creates a barrier between freedom and the apparently uneducated status of the speaking character here. Additionally, the word 'sloshes' makes it sound like freedom is a disturbance on the lives of enslaved peoples rather than a radical welcomed change. It evokes images of a calm body of water being disturbed and becoming rough and full of waves, this imagery could render freedom in a poor light rather than something of salvation. Additionally, the phrase 'us cullud folks' gives a sense of universality to both this experience and 
